2026-07-25 | RQ015-plan-v0-unit-correction | PLAN_AMENDED_STILL_PROPOSED_NO_COMPUTE | PI question exposed a unit ambiguity in the trigger evidence; measured both units and amended the plan. The earlier 23.2%/54.5% figures are FRAME x AGENT level. Case-level aggregation (38,228 cases, mean 96.7 frames): >=1 estimable value 96.01%, >=5 89.27%, >=10 82.76%, >=20 70.19%, >=5 strongly estimable (err<0.3) 73.28%, ZERO estimable values only 3.99%, cases with >=50%% of frames estimable only 8.64%, mean within-case estimable fraction 23.37%. So the corpus is NOT wasted at case level; estimability is sparse WITHIN each case. Harm is therefore unit-dependent: frame/anchor-level products (RQ009 M3 6.4M-anchor training and envelopes, RQ014 rolling per-tau readouts, RQ011B moment monitor) lose about half their rows, whereas episode/case-level products are far less affected PROVIDED summaries use estimable frames only -- which is exactly RQ007-KC-C3 (all-valid vs interaction-active summary means differ ~0.26 rad, ~22%% of cases flip strict sign, estimability weighting reduces flips to ~7%%). Second measurement: estimability is NOT mainly proximity-gated -- by inter-agent distance the estimable rate is 44.50%% below 5 m but flat at ~22%% for every bin beyond 5 m, and the <5 m bin is only 3.8%% of frames contributing ~7%% of estimable values; so what determines frame-level estimability is an open question now added to Phase A as the key evidence for 'repairable vs intrinsic identification limit'. Plan amended: new sections 2.4b/2.4c, Phase A expanded to dual-unit profiling, estimability-predictor analysis, and RQ007-KC-C3-based episode summary rules; checksums regenerated.
